# Supabase — copy this file to .env and fill in your project's values.
# Settings → API in the Supabase dashboard.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-public-key

# Legal documents and support contact.
#
# scripts/build-legal.mjs renders legal/*.html into public/legal/ with these
# substituted, and src/lib/legal.ts builds the app's links from the same values,
# so the documents and the links can't disagree.
#
# VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL is published: it is the privacy contact in the policy, and
# the support address in both store listings. A release build refuses to render
# without it (`build-legal --strict`), because a policy telling regulators to
# email a placeholder is worse than a failed build.
VITE_SITE_URL=https://macros-track.netlify.app
VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@your-domain.example

# External food lookups run server-side in the `food-search` Edge Function, so
# their API keys are NOT set here (they'd be exposed in the browser bundle).
# Set them as function secrets instead, e.g. the optional USDA key (free —
# https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup.html; defaults to DEMO_KEY if unset):
#   supabase secrets set USDA_API_KEY=your-key
# and the optional Open Food Facts account (skips OFF's anonymous rate limit;
# OFF has no API key — auth is HTTP Basic; requests stay anonymous if unset):
#   supabase secrets set OFF_USERNAME=your-off-user OFF_PASSWORD=your-off-password
#
# The national food-composition tables (ANSES-Ciqual, CoFID, CREA) need no key:
# they live in our own database and the function reads them with the anon key
# the Edge Runtime injects.

# Service role key — ONLY for scripts/import-reference-foods.mjs, which loads
# data/reference/*.csv into public.reference_foods.
#
# This key bypasses RLS. It is deliberately not a VITE_ variable: anything
# prefixed VITE_ is inlined into the browser bundle, and shipping this one would
# hand every visitor full read/write access to every user's data. Set it in your
# shell (or as the SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY repository secret, which is what
# the supabase-deploy CI job uses) — never in .env.
#   SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co \
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key \
#     pnpm run data:reference:import

# RevenueCat publishable SDK keys — what makes Pro buyable in the native apps.
#
# One per store, from the RevenueCat dashboard under Project → API keys → "App
# specific keys" (they start with `appl_` and `goog_`). These are *publishable*:
# they can read offerings and start a purchase, which the store then verifies
# itself, so shipping them in the bundle is how RevenueCat is designed to work.
# The **secret** key is a different value entirely and must never be set here —
# entitlements are decided server-side from the webhook, not by the client.
#
# Leave them unset and the paywall reports purchases as unavailable instead of
# failing on the first tap, which is what CI does. Browsers ignore these two
# entirely and use VITE_REVENUECAT_WEB_KEY below.
#
# The entitlement identifier must be `pro` (src/lib/purchases.ts), and the three
# products must be attached to it and offered through the *current* offering:
#   macrotrack_pro_monthly, macrotrack_pro_yearly, macrotrack_pro_lifetime
# See the "Pro subscription" section of the README for the webhook half.
# VITE_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY=appl_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# VITE_REVENUECAT_ANDROID_KEY=goog_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# RevenueCat Web Billing key (`rcb_`) — what makes Pro buyable in the browser.
#
# A third publishable key, from RevenueCat → Project → API keys, issued once Web
# Billing is enabled and connected to your Stripe account. Purchases made with it
# arrive through the *same* revenuecat-webhook as the app stores, as
# `store: 'STRIPE'` — which is the whole reason Web Billing was chosen over a
# direct Stripe integration: one webhook to secure, one table as the source of
# truth. A web purchase unlocks Pro in the native apps too, since entitlement is
# read from public.subscriptions rather than from any SDK.
#
# Unset, the paywall says purchases aren't available in a browser and points at
# the mobile apps. That is what CI and the e2e suite build.
#
# Note for the EU: with Web Billing you are (via Stripe) the merchant of record
# for consumer VAT, unlike App Store and Play sales where Apple and Google are.
# Enable Stripe Tax, or use a merchant-of-record vendor, before selling.
# VITE_REVENUECAT_WEB_KEY=rcb_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Show the "subscribe on the web" link inside the NATIVE apps. Off unless `1`.
#
# Apple 3.1.1 and Google Play Billing require store billing for purchases made
# inside an app. Both stores have been compelled to permit an external *link* in
# some regions — the US after the Epic injunctions, the EU under the DMA — and
# src/lib/purchases/externalPurchase.ts restricts the link to those storefronts.
#
# Do NOT set this until Apple has granted your app the
# `com.apple.developer.storekit.external-purchase-link` entitlement for the same
# regions. A link without it is a rejection, and possibly a removal. Setting this
# also makes scripts/patch-ios-project.mjs write the matching
# SKExternalPurchaseLink key into Info.plist — verify both key names against
# Apple's current documentation first; this is the fastest-moving corner of the
# review guidelines.
# VITE_EXTERNAL_PURCHASE_LINK=1

# Apple Developer Team ID — for running the iOS app on a *device* from Xcode.
#
# Not needed for the web app, for Android, or for the iOS simulator. Only
# device builds are signed, and Capacitor's template ships an empty team, so
# without this Xcode refuses with "Signing for 'App' requires a development
# team." Find it at https://developer.apple.com/account under Membership
# details — 10 uppercase letters and digits. A free Apple ID has one too.
#
# `scripts/patch-ios-project.mjs` reads it and writes it into the generated
# project on every `pnpm run sync:native`, which is what makes it stick: ios/ is
# gitignored and regenerated, so setting the team in Xcode's UI is undone by the
# next sync. CI uses the same name, as a repository secret.
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=XXXXXXXXXX
